{{Life in Balisca}}

'''Marriage in Balisca''' differs significantly from other countries; the hierarchical legal framework of marriage, with its associated rights and obligations, was progressively dismantled in the aftermath of the [[Baliscan Revolution]], effectively eliminating state-defined marital rights, and state-imposed divorce procedures. Today, "marriage" is widely considered a private cultural or religious formality between consenting adults, with no official recognition of [[W:Marriage#Religion|religious weddings]]. The absence of state regulation has fostered a culture of [[W:Free love|free love]], in which partnerships are viewed as fluid and adaptable, with an emphasis on [[W:Consent|consent]], mutual respect, and open communication. Baliscans embrace a wide range of relationship structures, including monogamous partnerships, [[w:Polyamory|polyamorous relationships]], [[W:Group marriage|group marriage]] arrangements, and platonic partnerships. The focus is on ethical and consensual relationships, rather than adherence to rigid social/religious norms. 

Traditional marriage has largely been replaced by the '''civil solidarity pact''' ({{langx|bl|pacto civil de solidaridad}}), commonly known as a '''''PACS''''' ({{IPA|bl|paks|pron}}), a [[W:Contractual|contractual]] form of [[W:Domestic partnership|domestic partnership]] between two or more adults for organizing their joint life. PACS are purely voluntary, entered into by the free will of the individuals involved. Individuals have complete freedom to define the terms of their partnership agreements; this includes anything from financial arrangements and shared property to child-rearing responsibilities and mutual support obligations. As such, it brings rights and responsibilities, but is less rigid than a traditional [[W:Marriage|marriage]], and ensures equal access for consensual partnerships deemed "non-traditional" (i.e. [[W:Same-sex relationships|same-sex relationships]], [[W:Casual relationships|casual relationships]], group marriages). 

From a legal standpoint, a PACS is a contract drawn up between the two or [[W:Group marriage|more]] individuals, which is stamped and registered by the clerk of their [[Communes of Balisca|commune]]. In some areas, partners signing a PACS have the option of undergoing a formal ceremony at the [[W:Cabildo (council)|cabildo]] identical to that of [[W:Civil marriage|civil marriage]]. Since 1978, individuals who have registered a PACS are no longer considered ''[[W:Single person|single]]'' in terms of their [[W:Marital status|civil status]]; their birth records will be amended to show their status as ''{{Linktext|pacsé}}''. In the event of disputes or disagreements, partners are able to seek mediation from [[Reconciliation council (Balisca)|reconciliation council]] within their community or from [[W:Voluntary associations|voluntary associations]] specializing in dispute resolution. Mediation typically focuses on facilitating communication and finding mutually agreeable solutions, rather than imposing legal judgments.

According to the 2025 Demographic Report by the [[Instituto Confederal de Stadística]] (ICS), the number of civil marriages in Balisca had fallen each year since 1978. 86,000 civil marriages took place in 2024, a decline of 5.9% from 2023. However, the report found that the number of partnerships getting PACS had increased every year during the same period. There was a 29% increase in PACS between 2021 and 2022 and a 25% increase between 2022 and 2023. For the first 9 months of 2024, 187,389 PACS were signed compared to 148,448 in 2023. The report found that one PACS in 10 had been dissolved (less than divorces for couples married for the same period, for which one marriage in three will be dissolved by divorce or separation after the first 3 years during which most signed PACS are dissolved before becoming more stable than marriages).
 
